From 5968ad9eef70bd926590b77d0c2f4d83cd558d3e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: The browser Authors <noreply@browser.invalid>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 03:25:58 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 36/61] linux-parity

A Linux build presenting Windows (see the user-agent commit) exposes and
answers what Windows Chrome does: Web Share, whose service makes the checks
Windows makes and then leaves the share pending, as Windows does while its
share dialog waits for a choice; Web Bluetooth with a Windows desktop's
adapter, available once that adapter is up, shortly after Chrome's start-up
is complete (the first visible page loaded and idle) as on Windows, where
calls made earlier wait for it
(content/browser/bluetooth/windows_bluetooth_adapter.h); WebAuthn's client
capabilities as a Windows 11 desktop lists them: Windows Hello as the
user-verifying platform authenticator, hybrid transport once the same
adapter is up, and Secure Payment Confirmation; with a US layout, the
keyboard layout map of Windows' US layout (IntlBackslash writes a backslash,
no IntlYen); the CSS system colors Windows maps to its native colors, at
Windows 10 and 11's defaults and in light mode as there, with Highlight
unblended as Windows' theme takes it, and the CSS system fonts menu,
small-caption and status-bar as Windows 11's Segoe UI at 12 px; a mouse, as
Windows reports one even where none is attached, so (pointer: fine) and
(hover: hover) hold on a server with no input devices; date and time inputs
whose format joins the date and the time with a space, as Windows does, not
ICU's comma; on a host with no audio devices, the microphone and speaker a
Windows desktop lists before permission (a persona's counts still replace
them); drive letters and UNC paths in URLs read as file paths in its
renderers, as Chrome on Windows reads them (a link to "c:/x" is
file:///C:/x; url/url_file.h); WebRTC's UDP sockets bound in Windows'
ephemeral port range, 49152 to 65535, from a random start, unless a policy
sets a range; and packets that start with Windows' TTL and IPv6 hop limit,
128, not Linux's 64, on every TCP and UDP socket the network service opens;
and IndexedDB's disk usage as Windows counts it, where a file LevelDB holds
open for writing lists at its size when last synced (the LOG at 0, the
write-ahead log without its unsynced tail): LevelDB's env keeps those sizes
(third_party/leveldatabase/env_chromium.h), and StorageManager.estimate()
and StorageBucket.estimate() report what a Windows machine reports;
and the C runtime's math as Chrome on Windows has it where pages read the
results (CSS math functions, DOMMatrix, transforms): sin, cos, tan, asin,
acos, atan, atan2, exp, log and hypot, and the float versions of the first
nine (layout-time calc(), colour conversions), from third_party/ucrt_math,
which returns the Universal C Runtime's bits in its FMA3 mode (derived
from AMD's
AOCL-LibM, BSD-3-Clause), through ui/gfx/geometry/platform_math.h; canvas
and SVG matrices (AffineTransform), CSSSkew.toMatrix(), the PointerEvent
tilt and angle conversions and transform decomposition take them too.
The library's self-test, which decides once per process, runs in the
default floating-point environment (its first caller may be an audio
thread with denormals flushed to zero).
A Blob or File made with endings "native" gets CR LF, Windows' native
line endings (BlobData::AppendText): a page reads the convention from
the blob's size, which differs by operating system.
file: URLs take the rest of Windows' rules where drive-letter strings
already did (url::WindowsDriveLetterUrlsApply()): a file: URL with no
drive letter and other than three slashes has a UNC host, a path
resolved against a drive-letter base keeps the drive, and "/z:/bar"
against a file base is a drive path.
Input conventions are Windows': WebView::ApplyWebPreferences() sets
Windows' editing behaviour (Selection.modify() by word stops after the
space, a caret placed above or below a block's lines keeps its column),
a double click selects the space after the word, the context menu
comes on mouse up, and the middle button starts autoscroll and never
pastes the selection; in editing_behavior.cc Alt+Backspace is Undo,
Ctrl+Shift+Backspace is unbound, and a character that arrives with
Ctrl down is inserted (Windows' AltGr is Ctrl+Alt; only a non-ASCII
character with Ctrl and no Alt, which a Linux keyboard can send and a
Windows one cannot, is still dropped). The context-menu key opens the
menu when it is released, and a scrollbar thumb dragged far off its
track snaps back. The network service's own process reads
drive-letter URLs as the renderers do, so a redirect's Location that
resolves to a file: URL on Windows fails here too. The browser process
keeps Linux's reading and opens a scope for one thread
(url::ScopedWindowsDriveLetterUrls, url/windows_drive_letter_urls.h)
around the two places where it resolves a service worker's string: the
Location of a worker's redirect and the Service-Worker-Allowed header.
Nothing changes on Windows or with --use-chromium-defaults, except that
utility processes (the network service among them) now receive
--use-chromium-defaults on every platform, as the GPU process does.
