+Name: ucrt_math
+Short Name: ucrt_math
+URL: https://github.com/amd/aocl-libm-ose
+Version: 5.3.2
+Revision: 29fd054f383e6c5e2dec2fce781d5220059f1836
+License: BSD-3-Clause
+License File: LICENSE
+Security Critical: yes
+Shipped: yes
+Update Mechanism: Static.HardFork
+
+Description:
+Double-precision sin, cos, tan, asin, acos, atan, atan2, exp, log and hypot,
+and the float sin, cos, tan, asin, acos, atan, atan2, exp and log, that
+return, bit for bit, what the Windows Universal C Runtime (ucrtbase.dll, x64)
+returns in its default FMA3 mode. Plain C99 with explicit fma(); no assembly
+or CPU-specific code. A Linux build presenting Windows uses them through
+ui/gfx/geometry/platform_math.h, so CSS math functions and DOMMatrix answer
+as Chrome on Windows does.
+
+Local Modifications:
+Derived from AMD AOCL-LibM at the revision above: sin, cos and tan translated
+from the FMA3 assembly into C; cos and tan below pi/4, exp's polynomial and
+small-argument path, and the fused steps of asin, acos, atan and atan2 fitted
+against ucrtbase.dll used as a black box; special values built from bit
+patterns as UCRT returns them. Every change is tied to a measured difference
+and has a test that checks it. ucrt_math_selftest() checks a build against
+known outputs.
